{
  "id": "ENT088",
  "title": "The Font Committee",
  "family": "fiction",
  "category": "creative-work struggles",
  "premise": "A creator wants one readable caption, but each letter insists on using a different decorative costume. After the words become impossible to fit, the creator gives every letter the same simple outfit and lets only the key word wear a small hat, making the joke readable at a glance.",
  "hook": "Every letter wants the leading role.",
  "visible_action": "Letters arrive in ornate costumes, bump off the line, change into simple forms, and settle; one key word gains a tiny hat.",
  "intended_takeaway_or_theme": "Hierarchy can give a detail emphasis without decorating everything.",
  "gate": "Use original letterforms or licensed fonts; test actual readability rather than claiming universal accessibility.",
